<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domitius Domitianus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 297</span> · Also known as Lucius Domitius Domitianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Domitius Domitianus was a usurper who proclaimed himself emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 297, probably in reaction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s tax reforms. Coins were struck in his name at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and the corrector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurelius-achilleus">Aurelius Achilleus</a> supported him. He died in December 297, and Diocletian crushed the revolt after a long siege of Alexandria in 298.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domitius-domitianus.jpg" alt="Domitius Domitianus" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nummus_Domitius_Domitianus_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
